{
  "headline": "Ghana National Research Fund to prioritize local solutions for national challenges",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "The Ghana National Research Fund (GNRF) intends to prioritize research into food security, environmental protection, health care, and technological innovation to address development challenges and reduce dependence on external research funding, according to officials in Accra. The announcement was made by Professor Marian Dorcas Quain, the Deputy Director-General of the Council for Scientific and Industrial Research (CSIR), and Professor Shamsudin Ziblim, the Deputy Administrator of the Ghana Research Fund, during a Scientific Conference and Innovation Fair 2026. Speaking on the topic of “Research Funding and Implementation in Ghana,” Professor Quain stated that the country requires a coordinated research system focused on practical solutions and specific challenges. The fund is expected to support researchers working on home-grown solutions, such as climate-smart crop varieties, sustainable natural resource management, emerging diseases, and innovations in science, technology, and engineering."
    },
    {
      "text": "Professor Quain noted that investing in agricultural research is essential for improving food production, strengthening food security, and developing seeds capable of withstanding changing weather conditions to increase yields. Regarding environmental protection, Quain said the fund would support research into sustainable natural resource management, specifically addressing environmental damage caused by illegal mining, known as galamsey. The fund will also support health-related research to assist scientists in responding to emerging diseases. Additionally, Quain indicated that investment in STEM, ICT, and engineering is necessary to stimulate innovation, while research in the humanities and social sciences could provide evidence-based solutions to economic development, corruption, and other societal challenges."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Ghana to host signing of UN trade convention on October 26, 2026",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Ghana is scheduled to host the signing of the United Nations Convention on Negotiable Cargo Documents, referred to as the Accra Convention, on October 26, 2026. The convention aims to modernize trade documentation and improve the efficiency of international commerce by establishing a legal framework for negotiable cargo documents in both paper and electronic formats for goods transported via road, rail, air, and sea. During a media engagement organized by the Ghana Shippers’ Authority (GSA) in Accra on October 15, 2026, Stella A. Ansah, the Director of the Foreign Trade and Negotiations Directorate at the Ministry of Trade, Agribusiness and Industry, announced the event on behalf of sector Minister Elizabeth Ofosu-Adjare. The engagement was intended to sensitize the media to the significance of the convention and Ghana’s role in hosting the ceremony. The signing is expected to involve representatives from governments, international organizations, and the global trading community. Professor Ransford Edward Gyampo, the Chief Executive Officer of the GSA, noted that modernizing trade documentation is a response to increasing digitalization and complex global supply chains."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Trump calls Ukraine’s renewed strikes on Russian oil refineries ‘a disgrace’",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "U.S. President Donald Trump has called Ukraine’s renewed strikes on Russian oil refineries “a disgrace” and criticized Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelenskyy following the collapse of an energy ceasefire. Trump stated that Zelenskyy is “more difficult to deal with than [Russian President Vladimir] Putin.” The criticism follows Ukraine's decision to resume attacks on Russian oil infrastructure after Zelenskyy announced Kyiv was abandoning a ceasefire unilaterally declared by Trump on October 11, 2026. Ukraine had briefly halted its attacks to demonstrate readiness for peace talks, but Russia had continued its strikes on Ukrainian power plants. Tensions between Washington and Kyiv have escalated over a deal made by the U.S. president to import diesel from Moscow and curbs on Ukraine's battlefield satellite network. Zelenskyy, speaking at an EU summit in Brussels, said he could not accept the diesel deal because a gas agreement could follow, stating, “If I don't stand up to this... who else will?”"
    },
    {
      "text": "During the EU summit in Brussels, Zelenskyy addressed the need for funding to help Ukraine through its fifth winter since the 2022 Russian invasion. He told the European Council that the war will cost €155bn (£131bn; $174bn) next year, noting that at least €70bn is required from the EU for defense and weapons. Zelenskyy also stated that while Ukraine has the capacity to produce up to three times more drones, the country lacks sufficient money for that production. Arriving at the summit on Friday, Zelenskyy expressed that it was good to be back among allies, referring to EU leaders as “real leaders.”"
    }
  ]
}

