[
  {"text": "At least four people died and two others suffered severe burns while battling wildfires in the Bromo Tengger Semeru National Park in East Java, Indonesia, officials said on Friday. According to a Forestry Ministry statement, the victims were trapped by fast-spreading flames and thick smoke during what the ministry described as the country's worst wildfires in a decade. Jules Abraham Abast, a spokesperson for the East Java police, stated the victims were part of a 12-member firefighting team composed of local volunteers, members of the community fire brigade, and national park personnel. The ministry noted that the incident highlights the high-risk nature of forest and land fire extinguishing operations. By the evening of October 15, 2026, the number of satellite-detected hotspots in Indonesia had reached 799, an increase from 372 the previous day, though officials noted that not all hotspots necessarily represent individual fires."}
